Perfect! I've confirmed the issue. The test shows:
- When using `--no-v` with alias `v: ['verbose']`, it sets `v: false` but `verbose` remains `true` (the default)
- The expected behavior is for both `v` and `verbose` to be `false`

Now let me analyze the root cause in the code more carefully and implement the fix.

Looking at the `parseRawArgs` function again:

```ts
// Handle --no- prefixed arguments by preprocessing
const processedArgs: string[] = [];
const negatedFlags: Record<string, boolean> = {};

for (let i = 0; i < args.length; i++) {
  const arg = args[i]!;

  if (arg === "--") {
    // Pass through -- and everything after
    processedArgs.push(...args.slice(i));
    break;
  }

  // Handle --no-flag syntax
  if (arg.startsWith("--no-")) {
    const flagName = arg.slice(5);
    negatedFlags[flagName] = true;
    continue;
  }

  processedArgs.push(arg);
}
```

This extracts `--no-*` flags and stores them separately, then later applies them:

```ts
// Apply negated flags
for (const [name] of Object.entries(negatedFlags)) {
  (out as any)[name] = false;
}

// Propagate values between aliases
for (const [alias, main] of aliasToMain.entries()) {
  if ((out as any)[alias] !== undefined && (out as any)[main] === undefined) {
    (out as any)[main] = (out as any)[alias];
  }
  if ((out as any)[main] !== undefined && (out as any)[alias] === undefined) {
    (out as any)[alias] = (out as any)[main];
  }
}
```

The issue is that when `--no-v` is processed, it sets `out['v'] = false`, but since `verbose` has a default value of `true`, the propagation logic skips it because it's not `undefined`. The fix requires resolving negated flags through the alias map so that when a flag is negated, all its aliases get the negated value applied as well.

